! jb_ax5b "I-Box 5", routine 1258 "CHK TIME CLOCK AND INTERVAL TIMER",
! TEST 1 -- the TIME CLOCK half.  PDF pp.5-6, three contiguous blocks:
! the 158 preamble at 014002.00-014004.40 plus its CNOP, the routine's
! four XW constants at 014006.00-014011.00, and TEST 1 proper at
! 014012.00-014033.40 (entry label I581), 36 unbroken half-word rows
! filling the whole of PDF p.6.  Transcribed VERBATIM at the listing's
! own addresses and checked bit-exactly by
! verify_ax5b_1258_test1_listing.py.
!
! WHERE IT IS.  Routine 1258 does NOT sit in the tail of the deck where
! its number would put it: it is the FIRST listing block, PDF pp.5-7,
! immediately after the prose write-up.  These listing pages carry no
! printed page numbers, so everything here cites PDF pages.
!
! WHAT IT IS.  The deck's own write-up, PDF p.5, printed above the code:
!
!   ----I258---CHK TIME CLOCK AND INTERVAL TIMER.   (see below on I vs 1)
!       -THIS TEST IS COMPOSED OF TWO ROUTINES
!       -WHICH CHECK THE FOLLOWING FUNCTIONS.
!       -TEST 1 IS A CURSORY CHECK OF
!       -THE TIME CLOCK TO SEE THAT
!       -IT STEPS CORRECTLY WITHIN
!       -THE CORRECT INTERVAL OF TIME.
!       -TEST 2 CHECKS THAT THE INTERVAL
!       -TIMER STEPS WITHIN THE
!       -ALLOTTED AMOUNT OF TIME, AND
!       -THAT EACH POSITION CAN HOLD
!       -BOTH A ONE AND A ZERO.
!
! TEST 2 (014034.00-014063.40, PDF p.7) is already transcribed as
! programs/ax5b_1258_test2.strap.  This file is its other half, and the
! two together are the whole of routine 1258.
!
! WHY IT MATTERS.  This model has no time clock at all -- not a stub,
! not a register: nothing.  This block is the acceptance test for
! building one, written by the people who had one.
!
! -------------------------------------------------------------------
! WHERE THE ONE CUT IS, AND WHY IT IS FORCED
! -------------------------------------------------------------------
! 014005.00 is NOT transcribed.  Its source line, read at 600 dpi, is
!
!       I58ID   %IQSZ*DD%BU,64,8*,I258      Z
!
! CORRECTED 2026-09-24.  This line was transcribed as
! "1581D ... ,1258", with digits.  Re-measured at 600 dpi, the label's
! FIRST and FOURTH characters are both the letter I (12 and 17 px
! against a 30-42 px band for this chain's digit 1, which carries an
! upper-left flag and a base serif that the bare stroke does not), and
! the operand is I258.  Cross-checked against the identical ident rows
! for the sibling routines, which read "I46ID ... ,I246" and
! "I74ID ... ,I274".  The banner above is "----I258---" for the same
! reason.  Full evidence and the repo-wide scope in
! docs/listing_typography.md.
!
! NOTHING THIS FILE CHECKS IS AFFECTED, and that is worth saying
! precisely rather than reassuringly: this row prints NO machine code
! and is deliberately not transcribed, so no bit moves.  But it is the
! ONE row in the file where the glyph reading would have reached
! emitted bits had it been checked -- the operand is the routine
! number, and a digit reading would have deposited the wrong word.
! The cut that was forced for an unrelated reason is what kept the
! error harmless.
!
! where % is this printer's "(" and * stands here for the closing
! glyph it draws for ")" -- fixed by the same page's
! "XW,%8*777777.40,0,0", which is the "(8)" radix prefix this assembler
! already accepts.  So the line reads
! "(IQSZ)DD(BU,64,8),I258   Z": a call of the SSIP/DCP print-formatting
! macro %IQSZ, the routine's IDENT word.  docs/archival_inventory.md
! already records %IQSZ/%IQS* as macros with no counterpart in this
! model, and notes that every routine's ident line carries one.
!
! THE LISTING PRINTS NO MACHINE CODE FOR IT.  The location column says
! 014005.00; the machine-code column is blank.  So there is nothing to
! check there, and writing "DD(BU,64,8),1258" instead would assert a row
! shape the page does not print and deposit a word the page does not
! show.  The cut is therefore a SLC from 014004.40 to 014006.00, and the
! ident word enters as a SYN, I58ID, because 014002.00's LX names it.
!
! Everything else in 014002.00-014033.40 is transcribed, unbroken.
!
! -------------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE TEST DOES, AND WHY IT IS SHAPED THIS WAY
! -------------------------------------------------------------------
! Reference Manual printed p.35 (PDF 38), read first-hand:
!
!   "This clock consists of a number 36 bits long which is continually
!    stepped up by pulses originating from the same 1024 cycle per
!    second oscillator which controls the updating of the interval
!    timer.  The two clocks are updated consecutively in the same time
!    interval.  The leftmost 26 bits of the time clock measure time in
!    seconds.  A full cycle is about 777 days."
!
!   "The value of the clock occupies bits 28-63 of location 1.  Each
!    time the oscillator delivers a pulse it is read out, incremented by
!    one, USING THE INDEX ADDER, and returned to core storage."
!
!   "Its setting, however, cannot be altered under program control.  Any
!    attempt to store into the right half of location 1 is suppressed,
!    and indicator AD, address invalid, is turned on."
!
! Bits 28-63 of a word are exactly the COUNT field (28-45) and the
! REFILL field (46-63) of an INDEX WORD.  That is the whole trick of
! this block: word 1 is read as ONE index word with LX, and the 36-bit
! clock comes back split across two index fields that half-word
! instructions can reach separately --
!
!   SR (STORE REFILL, Ref. Manual p.24): "The refill field, bits 46-63,
!   of the index word specified by J replaces bits 0-17 of the half word
!   specified by bits 0-18 of the effective address.  Bits 18-24 of the
!   half word are set to zero."
!
!   SC (STORE COUNT, p.24): the same sentence with "count field, bits
!   28-45".
!
! So SR moves the clock's LOW 18 bits and SC its HIGH 18 bits, each into
! the VALUE position of some other index word, where KV (COMPARE VALUE,
! p.24: "bits 0-24 ... compared with ... bits 0-24 of the index word
! specified by J") can compare them.  The four scratch words are $X2
! (old low), $X3 (old high), $X4 (new low), $X5 (new high) -- and
! "Z,$X2" zeroes them by addressing index register 2 as storage word 18,
! which is why those rows assemble to 22/23/24/25 octal.
!
! THE THREE ASSERTIONS, each with its own error text, quoted from p.6:
!
!   1. IT STEPS AT ALL, within one millisecond (I581/I582).  Snapshot
!      the clock, then spin a 256-count CB re-reading word 1 and asking
!      whether the low half still equals the snapshot.  BZXE branches
!      out when XE is OFF -- when they DIFFER -- and the comment on that
!      row is the deck's own "-IT STEPPED."  Fall out of the 256 tries
!      and the report is
!          "-TIME CLOCK FAILS TO STEP IN 1 MSEC."
!      One millisecond is the deck's statement of the rate; the manual's
!      1,024 steps per second is one step per 0.9766 ms.
!
!   2. THE LOW HALF WENT UP BY EXACTLY ONE (I583/I584).  V+I,$X2,1.0
!      adds one to the saved old low half and I584's KV,$X2,$X4 compares
!      it against the new low half.  Failure:
!          "-BITS 46-63 ARE NOT CORRECT AFTER"
!          "-TIME CLOCK STEPPED."
!      Those bit numbers ARE the refill field's, printed by the deck.
!      AND THE ASSIGNMENT OF MESSAGE TO BLOCK DOES NOT REST ON READING
!      PIXEL POSITIONS: I584 compares $X2 against $X4 and I585 compares
!      $X3 against $X5 in the CODE column, SR wrote $X2/$X4 and SC wrote
!      $X3/$X5, and the manual independently calls refill 46-63 and
!      count 28-45.  Deck text and manual corroborate each other; the
!      same corroboration TEST 2 gets from its "IX 1"/"IX 2"/"IX 3".
!
!   3. THE HIGH HALF IS RIGHT, CARRY INCLUDED (I585).  KV,$X3,$X5
!      compares the saved old high half against the new one.  Failure:
!          "-BITS 28-45 ARE NOT CORRECT AFTER"
!          "-TIME CLOCK STEPPED."
!      Again the deck names the count field's own bit numbers over the
!      SC rows at 014015.40/014022.00.
!
! THE CARRY PATH DOES NOT REACH THE FIELD THAT IS COMPARED, and this is
! a finding, not a guess.  BZXVZ at 014023.00 branches past the carry
! step while XVZ is OFF, i.e. while the incremented low half is NONZERO;
! only a wrap (old low = 2**18-1) falls through to 014023.40's
! C+I,$X3,1.0.  But Reference Manual printed p.25 defines that
! instruction as
!
!   "ADD IMMEDIATE TO COUNT (C+I): Bits 0-17 of the address part of the
!    instruction are added to the COUNT field, BITS 28-45, of the index
!    word specified by J."
!
! -- while 014026.00's KV compares bits 0-24, the VALUE field, which is
! where SC put the old high half.  The carry lands in a field the
! comparison never reads.  The block therefore reports a spurious
! "-BITS 28-45 ARE NOT CORRECT" on the one pass in 2**18 where the low
! half wraps (about once every 256 seconds of clock time, and only if
! the test is entered exactly then).  C+I and V+I differ by one letter
! in the source and one nibble in the printed encoding -- 1.07 00 versus
! 1.07 05, which the verifier's own injection demonstrates -- and V+I
! would have propagated the carry.  What the deck INTENDED is not
! recoverable from the page; that the encoding does what p.25 says it
! does is.  I585 is reached on both paths out of I584 (BXE,I585 when the
! low half checks out, fall-through into the SIC/B,SERS report
! otherwise), so nothing conditionally skips the affected block.
!
! NOTHING HERE STORES INTO WORD 1.  Unlike TEST 2, which sets the
! interval timer with SV,$X0,1.0 and so needs the interruption system
! disabled, TEST 1 only ever READS the clock (LX,$X0,1.0, twice).  That
! is forced: p.35 says the clock's setting "cannot be altered under
! program control", and a store into the right half of location 1 is
! suppressed with indicator AD turned on.  A cursory check is all the
! deck CAN do.
!
! THE CONTINUITY BLOCK IS UPDATE-ONLY.  TEST 1 closes with three rows --
! LX,$X13,IC258 / V+,$X13,BIT0 / SX,$X13,IC258 -- and no compare.  TEST 2
! does the same update with BIT1 and then verifies the word against
! ICK258.  ICK258's own row on PDF p.5 prints "600000.00 00 000000.00
! 00": bits 0 AND 1 set, which is "test 1 done" plus "test 2 done", read
! off the constant pool rather than inferred.  That also settles the
! symbol this file's 014033.00 names: the printer's letter O and digit
! 0 are only 15-22% apart in width and not worth resolving by eye
! (docs/listing_typography.md section 6), but the encoding is 24522 --
! one below
! TEST 2's BIT1 at 24523 -- and ICK258 shows bit 0 is the bit TEST 1
! sets.  BIT0, with a digit.
!
! THE BIT TABLE GAINS ITS BASE.  BIT1 -> 024523, BIT17 -> 024543 and
! BIT18 -> 024544 were read off TEST 2.  This row reads 024522 for BIT0,
! which is the 024522 + n rule's own origin.
!
! CNOP, at 014004.40's successor.  The listing prints NEITHER a location
! NOR machine code for the CNOP line: 014004.40 is the second half of
! word 014004, so the counter is already at the word boundary 014005.00
! and CNOP has nothing to fill.  It is kept as CNOP and never
! hand-expanded.  Because the transcript cuts at the very next row, the
! SLC that follows re-establishes the counter, so NO ROW IN THIS FILE
! WOULD MOVE if CNOP misbehaved -- the silence is witnessed by the page,
! not by this check.  verify_ax5b_160_test8c_disabled_listing.py is the
! file that witnesses both CNOP branches from assembled output.
!
! ENCODING ONLY.  Running this needs SSIP's harness (SEN/SERS/SSW/RET/
! IDF1), the %IQSZ print macro, and a time clock that ticks.  The model
! has none of the three.
!
! SUBSTITUTIONS: none in the label names, and the "none" is itself a
! correction.  This file used to read the deck's leading glyph as a
! digit (158, 1581, 1581D, 158K1, ...) and prefix an "L" to each,
! because this assembler reads a leading digit as an address; IC258 and
! ICK258 were spelled LIC258/LICK258 to match ax5b_1258_test2.strap,
! on the belief that this printer's letter I and digit 1 are the same
! glyph.  Measured at 600 dpi they are not, and C22-6531 printed p.10
! forbids a digit-leading symbol outright, so the page's own spellings
! -- I58, I581...I586, I58ID, I58K1, I58K2, IC258, ICK258 -- are legal
! STRAP-1 symbols as printed and are written here unaltered.  The
! measurement, and what it did and did not change, are in
! docs/listing_typography.md.  Nothing else is changed.

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------
! External symbols, SYN'd at the addresses THIS listing's own printed
! encodings give.  EVERY SYN HERE IS AN INPUT TO THE CHECK, NOT A
! WITNESS: the address was read out of the very row that names the
! symbol, so that row can only re-derive what was put in.  The real
! address checks are the thirteen rows whose targets are labels defined
! INSIDE this transcript.
!
! Each address was obtained by reading bit 18 out of the octal pair of
! the naming row, so each is itself a checkable claim.
!
! The SYN list is SHORTER than TEST 2's by four, and that is the reason
! this file carries the preamble and the constant pool: IC258, ICK258,
! I58K1 and I58K2 are DEFINED here, so 014004.00's Z, 014032.40's LX and
! 014033.40's SX are real address checks instead of TEST 2's "(ext)".
! It works the other way round too, and that is the more useful
! direction: ax5b_1258_test2.strap SYNs IC258, ICK258, I58K1 and
! I58K2 as unwitnessed inputs, and this file defines all four from
! their own printed constant-pool rows at exactly those addresses --
! including the two constants TEST 2's reload and bits-19-23 assertions
! rest on.
I58ID  SYN, (8)14005.0     ! from LX,$X1,I58ID     = 14005.02 10
DPET13  SYN, (8)1437.0      ! from SX,$X1,DPET13    = 1437.03 10
RET     SYN, (8)1306.40     ! from SIC,RET          = 1306.40 80
IDF1    SYN, (8)1443.0      ! from B,IDF1           = 1443.10 00
BIT0    SYN, (8)24522.0     ! from V+,$X13,BIT0     = 24522.32 B0
SEN     SYN, (8)1310.0      ! the SIC error-entry link
SENO    SYN, (8)1311.0      ! from SIC,SENO+.32     = 1311.40 80
SERS    SYN, (8)1304.0      ! the error-report routine
SSW     SYN, (8)1301.0      ! the SSIP return
!
! SENO's and DPET13's spellings do not follow from the labels above.
! The letter-I-versus-digit-1 half of what this note used to claim --
! that the two are one glyph on this printer -- is settled the other
! way and is why the labels are spelled I58..., above.  The letter O
! against digit 0 is a weaker result in the same direction: letter O
! runs 15-22% wider than digit 0 on both decks, so the two ARE
! separable, but on n = 3 calibration pairs (docs/listing_typography.md
! section 6, "moderate, direction only").  Both are SYNs, so nothing in
! this check or any injection depends on either spelling.  SENO follows
! ax5b_1258_test2.strap;
! DPET13 follows the spelling docs/microarchitecture_plan.md already
! carries for the same SSIP symbol in the PAU deck.

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------
! BLOCK A -- routine 158's preamble, PDF p.5, 014002.00-014004.40.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        SLC, (8)14002.0
I58    LX,  $X1, I58ID            ! -UPDATE IDENT.
        SX,  $X1, DPET13
        SIC, RET
        B,   IDF1
        Z,   IC258                 !             clear the continuity word
        BD,  I581                  !             ...and enter TEST 1 disabled
        CNOP                        !             already word-aligned: emits nothing
!
! ---- the cut: 014005.00, "%IQSZ*DD%BU,64,8*,1258  Z", the ident-word
!      print macro.  Location printed, machine code NOT printed.  See
!      the header.

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------
! BLOCK B -- routine 1258's constant pool, PDF p.5, 014006.00-014011.00.
! Four full words, each printing as two groups.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        SLC, (8)14006.0
IC258  XW,  0,0,0                  ! -CONTINUITY REG 1258.
ICK258 XW,  (8)600000.00,0,0       !             bits 0 and 1: both tests done
I58K1  XW,  (8)777777.40,0,0       !             all ones in bits 0-18 (TEST 2)
I58K2  XW,  (8)1.77,0,0            !             bit 17 plus bits 18-23 (TEST 2)

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------
! BLOCK C -- TEST 1 itself, the whole of PDF p.6, 014012.00-014033.40.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        SLC, (8)14012.0
!
! ---- snapshot the clock and split it -------------------------------
I581   LCI, $X1, 256               ! -TEST 1, CHECK TIME CLOCK STEPPING.
        LX,  $X0, 1.0               !             word 1 AS ONE INDEX WORD
        Z,   $X2                    !             word 18
        Z,   $X4                    !             word 20
        Z,   $X3                    !             word 19
        Z,   $X5                    !             word 21
        SR,  $X0, $X2               !             old bits 46-63 -> X2's value
        SC,  $X0, $X3               !             old bits 28-45 -> X3's value
!
! ---- does it step within one millisecond? --------------------------
I582   LX,  $X0, 1.0               !             re-read the clock
        SR,  $X0, $X4               !             new bits 46-63 -> X4's value
        KV,  $X4, $X2               !             new low half vs old low half
        BZXE, I583                 ! -IT STEPPED.      XE off = they differ
        CB,  $X1, I582             !             ...256 tries
        SIC, SEN
        B,   SERS                   ! -TIME CLOCK FAILS TO STEP IN 1 MSEC.
        B,   I586                  !             give up on TEST 1
!
! ---- old + 1 = new, with a carry between the halves ----------------
I583   SC,  $X0, $X5               !             new bits 28-45 -> X5's value
        V+I, $X2, 1.0               !             old low half + 1
        BZXVZ, I584                !             XVZ off = nonzero: no carry
        C+I, $X3, 1.0               !             the carry -- see the header
I584   KV,  $X2, $X4               !             old low + 1 vs new low
        BXE, I585
        SIC, SEN                    ! -BITS 46-63 ARE NOT CORRECT AFTER
        B,   SERS                   ! -TIME CLOCK STEPPED.
!
I585   KV,  $X3, $X5               !             old high vs new high
        BXE, I586
        SIC, SEN                    ! -BITS 28-45 ARE NOT CORRECT AFTER
        B,   SERS                   ! -TIME CLOCK STEPPED.
!
! ---- the exit, and the continuity update ---------------------------
I586   B,   $+1.0
        BD,  I581
        SIC, SENO+.32
        B,   SSW                    ! -TO SSIP.
        BD,  $+.32
!
        LX,  $X13, IC258           ! -UPDATE CONTINUITY CHECK.
        V+,  $X13, BIT0             !             test 1 done = bit 0
        SX,  $X13, IC258
        END, I58
